		<div class="post" id="post-2009648">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2009648)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2009648">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1777811&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605884042" alt="weizhong" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1777811)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;" >weizhong</a></span> <span class="odate time_1397079106 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Apr 2014 21:31</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2009648">
									

<p>This was a very close downvote for me. This is a <em>superb</em> attempt at a first SCP, and very commendable. The tone was excellent, and I think the idea was kind of cool. Ultimately, it came down to the execution and the ending twist.</p>
<p>My quibbles with it:</p>
<p>You blackbox names in the Special Containment Procedures. This is kind of counterproductive, because then nobody knows where this thing is, or where to consult the right people to handle the situation.</p>
<p>The next thing is personal in nature, so take it with a grain of salt. I personally feel that Level 2 is a little low in terms of clearance, as this would include practically all research staff, which kind of negates the value of the SCP being top secret.</p>
<p>Back to less subjective stuff. The initial circumstances of -3's recovery seem a little iffy to me. I think that this box having weird drawings is a little tenuous at best for Foundation containment. This kind of plays into the rest of the article, because I'm left considering how in the heck someone decided that this weird looking box had to be contained.</p>
<p>My biggest issue boils down to the final note. At times, the researcher seems slightly melodramatic, especially in the last sentence. It adds this air of melodrama and fanciful language that really contrasts with the rest of the article, and the note. This is present at other points in the note as well.</p>
<p>I'm also a bit mixed with how you finished it off. I think that listing the aspects of the procedure really hurt the prior effect of the &quot;Whoa, this is an ancient galaxy mover.&quot; You have this great twist moment with the revelation of the box, but then the article seems to drag on for another gasp through the procedures, leaving the reader with an overall mixed reception.</p>
<p>All in all, this was a great attempt. It just personally didn't work for me.</p>

							</div>

								
					
