					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/voct" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(392303); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=392303&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605884042" alt="Voct" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=392303)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/voct" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(392303); return false;" >Voct</a></span> <span class="odate time_1397087515 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Apr 2014 23:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2009733">
									

<p>What's more likely, and easier: that these Shang-era astronomers were able to reshape the universe but only did it on a scale of billions of galaxies? Or that they reshaped <em>the images of galaxies as they're seen from Earth</em> ?</p>
<p>Heinlein &#8212; I <em>think</em> it was Heinlein &#8212; did a story about this, actually. Astronomers realize that the brightest stars in the sky are all moving anomalously fast in weird directions, and after several days they're horrified to see that the brightest stars in the sky have rearranged themselves to spell the words &quot;BUY SNIVLEY'S SOAP&quot;.</p>
<p>And in his apartment, George Sniveley looks up at the sky, cackling over his success of his machine, and then realizes IT'S SPELLED WRONG, and has a heart attack out of sheer rage and frustration.</p>
<p>Three months later, the power company notices that he hasn't been paying his bill (because he's dead), and shuts off his electricity. Once the machine shuts off, the stars instantly switch back to their original positions. The actual stars hadn't moved, just their images as seen from Earth.</p>
<p>(but for those three months, Sniveley's Soap was the top-selling soap in the whole world.)</p>
<p>Even if we do assume that this is actually reshaping the positions of the galaxies instead of just their images, remember that <em>most of our galaxy would have the same vantage point of the modifications as we do</em>. The Zyglaxians would think &quot;oh, those arrows point to Zyglax! And look, there's a stick-figure image of a Zyglaxian - sort of crude, and the antlers are too small, but it's obviously intentional! And an image of Zyglax Prime and its ten planets!&quot;</p>
<p>Entities in <em>nearby</em> galaxies would also have a similar vantage point. And if you've never seen a humanoid bauplan, you won't be able to recognize one when you see a crude stick-figure image of one spelled out in billions of galaxies.</p>

							</div>

								
					
